# UST.G3AX Selected-Residue Graph-Ball Top-Functional Theorem

## Purpose

G3AV and G3AW identify the top functional needed for the six Gram entries,
but G3AP emits it only over `F_(101^6)`. This theorem proves the correct
characteristic-zero statement: on the entire G3AI/G3AJ graph-preserving
residue ball, that finite functional extends to one uniquely normalized
relative analytic functional.

The word "relative" is essential. G3AD proves that the convenient fixed
integer-gamma lift is not the selected graph-Prym member. G3AX therefore does
not promote that lift or select one point of the 123-dimensional graph ball.
It removes the functional ambiguity after, or while, that member is selected.

## Relative local model

Let

```text
M(g) = 64 + 16g + 33g^2 + 44g^3 + 89g^4 + 24g^5 + g^6.
```

Its reduction modulo 101 is irreducible and separable. The unramified base is

```text
O_0 = Z_101[g]/(M),       residue field k=F_(101^6).
```

Let `O'` be one complete finite integral extension carrying the common formal
graphs from G3AI and the G3AJ coefficient ball. G3AH and G3AJ identify that
ball as a rank-123 formal affine family. In local coordinates its complete
coefficient ring may be written

```text
R = O'[[u_1,...,u_123]].
```

Every point has the same G3AL/G3AP reduction. No particular characteristic-
zero point is selected here.

## Unit-minor family

Let `A(u)` be the 92,979 by 39,726 target-degree relation matrix formed from
the same graph-ball coefficient source. The G3AP rank witness selects 39,725
rows and every target column except

```text
j_* = 28267.
```

Write

```text
B(u)=A(u)[I,J],          c(u)=A(u)[I,j_*].
```

The reduction of `det B(u)` is the certified nonzero element encoded by
`748486604801`. It is therefore a unit in `R`, uniformly over the full fixed-
residue formal ball. Consequently

```text
t(u)[j_*] = 1,
t(u)[J]   = -B(u)^(-1)c(u)
```

defines one and only one normalized relative analytic functional.

It annihilates the selected rows by construction. G3AQ proves that the full
relative ordinary-top quotient is a free rank-one module with the selected
normalization class surviving. The selected-row quotient is already a free
rank-one direct summand. Since the full quotient is also free rank one, the
induced surjection between the two normalized lines is an isomorphism. Thus
`t(u)` annihilates all 92,979 rows.

Reduction modulo 101 gives exactly the emitted G3AP functional. This promotes
its source and normalization, not any individual finite-field entry as a
characteristic-zero number.

## Execution consequence

There is no longer a separate top-functional choice at each candidate member.
One may either:

1. bind a graph-ball member `u_*` and solve the displayed unit system once; or
2. retain `u` formally and evaluate the six G3AW contractions as analytic
   functions on the same ball used by the `beta_C` selection equations.

Both routes must use the same coefficient source for `A(u)`, the transported
vectors and `beta_C`. Reusing the G3AD fixed integer lift would violate the
already proved first-order graph obstruction.

## Proof boundary

G3AX proves relative existence, uniqueness, analytic dependence and reduction
compatibility. It does not select one graph-ball point, emit its 39,726
values, execute the six Gram contractions, prove Gram nondegeneracy, or emit
the meridian, `beta_C` or Hull-Strominger endpoint.

